      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(apps): call chat sessions threads",
        "body": "Related: #110933\nRelated: #110959\nRelated: #110973\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where users moving between the web Control UI and native Android, iOS, or macOS chat surfaces would see the same conversations called threads in one app and sessions in another.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nMirrors the completed web wording sweep mechanically across native chat labels, generated string inventories, and locale catalogs. Chat conversations are now threads; technical contracts such as Session ID, session keys, gateway and terminal sessions, cron targets, and Talk sessions remain sessions.\n\nThe Android generator also preserves an existing localized technical label when its incidental UI inventory entry is retired but the shared tool-display source remains live. That closes the regeneration edge exposed by this rename without adding a runtime compatibility path.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNative users now see consistent chat wording across platforms. Representative changes include:\n\n- Search sessions → Search threads\n- Rename session → Rename thread\n- Sessions → Threads on chat navigation and settings surfaces\n- Session missing → Thread missing\n\nTechnical session terminology remains unchanged where it describes protocol, storage, terminal, cron, or realtime-session concepts.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Native inventory gate: 4,856 entries, 21 locale artifacts, no drift.\n- Android i18n gate: 1,478 generated keys across 21 locales.\n- Apple i18n gate: 1,488 iOS keys, 5 phased macOS keys, and 84 InfoPlist files across 21 translated locales.\n- Android ktlint passed for app, benchmark, Wear, and wear-shared on Blacksmith Testbox tbx_01kxvqa51q91k778pc3jqf8nr4: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29664435406\n- test/scripts/android-app-i18n.test.ts: 20 tests passed, including the retained technical-label translation regression.\n- Source-blind artifact contract passed for new labels, retired labels, all Apple locales, and retained technical session terms.\n- OpenClawTypographyTests renamed thread-control test passed on Xcode 27 / iOS 26.5 simulator. The wider selected run also exposed two unrelated current-main source-guard failures: existing unbranded controls in the broad typography scan and a stale URL-handler formatting assertion. Neither failing assertion is changed by this patch.\n- git diff --check passed.\n- Final five-chunk autoreview completed with no accepted or actionable findings.\n\nThis is a text-only, multi-platform localization sweep; the before/after copy above and generated catalog diffs are the visual evidence rather than app screenshots for every native surface.\n\nAI-assisted: implementation, locale review, and validation were completed with Codex; the final diff was reviewed through the repository autoreview workflow.\n",
